<section xml:id="section-cyclic-groups">
  <title>Cyclic Groups</title>
  <p>
    In <xref ref="example-integers-modulo"/>, I talked about the group
    structure of <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m> (with addition as the operation).
    The integers modulo <m>n</m> is an example of an important type of
    group, which I will define in this section. I need a bit of setup
    to make the definition.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be a group and let <m>g \in G</m>. The
        <term>order</term> of the element <m>g</m> is the smallest
        natural number <m>d</m> such that <m>g^d = e</m>. If no such
        natural number exists, then we say that the order of <m>g</m>
        is infinite. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <definition xml:id="definition-generated">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be a group and let <m>g_i</m> be some elements of
        the group, for <m>i \in I</m> some indexing set. We say that
        <m>G</m> is <term>generated by</term> the elements <m>g_i</m>
        if all elements of <m>g</m> can be written as finite products
        of elements <m>g_i</m> and <m>g_i^{-1}</m>. A group is called
        <term>finitely generated</term> if it can be generated by a
        finite number of <m>g_i</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    Now I can make the main definition for this section.
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A group <m>G</m> is a <term>cyclic group</term> if it is
        generated by one element. Any element that generates the group
        is, unsurprisingly, a <term>generator</term> of the cyclic
        group. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    The integers modulo <m>n</m> are the first example of cyclic
    groups.  
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The group <m>\ZZ/n\ZZ</m> is a cyclic group with generator
        <m>\bar{1}</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        I need to prove that all elements of the group can be
        manufactured just using the generator and its inverse. Here, I
        can just do this by adding the generator to itself any number
        of times. I'll drop the overline notation for ease of reading
        here, but this is all arithmetic modulo <m>n</m>. 
        <md>
          <mrow>
            1 \amp = 1 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            2 \amp = 1 + 1 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            3 \amp = 1 + 1 + 1 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            4 \amp = 1 + 1 + 1 + 1 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            \vdots \amp = \vdots
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            n-2 \amp = 1 + 1 + 1 + \ldots + 1 \ \ (n-2 \ \ 1s) 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            n-1 \amp = 1 + 1 + 1 + \ldots + 1 \ \ (n-1 \ \ 1s) 
          </mrow>
          <mrow>
            0 = n \amp = 1 + 1 + 1 + \ldots + 1 \ \ (n \ \ 1s) 
          </mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>
        There are a finite number of steps here, and in each step, I
        get the number I want just by adding 1 to itself that many
        times. I also get the identity at the end, since <m>0</m> is
        equal to <m>n</m> modulo <m>n</m>. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    I can take the same argument and apply it to the integers
    themselves.
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The integers are a cyclic group generated by <m>1</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Take any positive integer <m>n</m>. Then <m>n</m> can be
        written as the sum of 1 with itself <m>n</m> times. Likewise,
        <m>-n</m> can be written as the sum of <m>-1</m> with itself
        <m>n</m> times. Finally, <m>0 = 1 -1</m>. This shows that all
        integers are finite sums of <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m>, which
        satisfies the definition and shows that the integers are
        generated by the element <m>1</m>. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
  <p>
    These are the important examples. I will discuss later that these
    are, in some sense, the only examples of cyclic groups. That is,
    every cyclic group will have the same structure as either the
    integers or the integers modulo <m>n</m>. What I mean, formally, by
    <sq>the same structure</sq> will be developed later. For now, I
    want to know the properties of cyclic groups. This is abstract
    algebra: once I define a thing, I want to investigate its
    properties, what I can prove about it. I'll prove one key property
    of cyclic groups.
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        All cyclic groups are abelian. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        Let <m>G</m> be the group and let <m>a</m> be the generator.
        Then, for any two groups elements, the can be written as
        <m>g = a^k</m> and <m>h = a^l</m> for some integers <m>k</m>
        and <m>l</m>. Then I can make this calculation.
        <me>
          gh = a^k a^l = a^{k+l} = a^{l+k} = a^la^k = hg 
        </me>
        This is a valid calculation because the integerchange of the
        sum is happening in the integers, where I know addition is
        commutative. The calculation shows that <m>G</m> is
        abelian, since <m>g</m> and <m>h</m> were chosen
        arbitrarily and the calculation demonstrates that I can
        reverse the order of their multiplication. 
      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
</section>
